{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 53, :comments ["<b>To these shall the land be divided, etc.</b> The verse's saying <b>לָאֵלֶּה</b> — our Rabbis of blessed memory disagreed concerning it in the chapter Yesh Nochalin (Bava Batra 117a), and this is their language: Rabbi Yoshiyah said: it was divided to those who came out of Egypt, as it is said: ״לִשְׁמוֹת מַטּוֹת אֲבוֹתָם יִנְחָלוּ״. Rather, what do I fulfill: ״לָאֵלֶּה״ etc.? לָאֵלֶּה — to exclude the minors, meaning that it should not be divided except to those who came out of Egypt from the age of twenty and up. And Rabbi Yonatan says: it was divided to those who entered the land, as it is said: ״לָאֵלֶּה תֵּחָלֵק הָאָרֶץ״. And what do I fulfill: ״לִשְׁמוֹת מַטּוֹת״? This inheritance is different, etc., and here the dead inherit, etc. — until here. According to the words of Rabbi Yonatan, the saying of ״לָאֵלֶּה״ comes to exclude the words of Rabbi Yoshiyah from the implication of the verse ״לִשְׁמוֹת מַטּוֹת״ etc." "And I saw one baraita (Sifrei), and this is their language: ״לָאֵלֶּה״ — sons of worthy and holy ones, to exclude the wicked among them who had no portion or inheritance. And they proved the matter from the sons of the spies and the complainers, and this line of reasoning follows the approach of Rabbi Yoshiyah, who said it was divided to those who came out of Egypt. Therefore, when it says ״לָאֵלֶּה״ — like these, who were all righteous, excluding the wicked who were among those who came out of Egypt, namely the spies, whose sons did not take their portion. And it appears that this derashah, which excludes the wicked, does not crowd out our derashah, which excludes those who came out of Egypt under the age of twenty, nor vice versa, for all of them are heard together from the statement ״כָּאֵלֶּה״ — whoever is not כָּאֵלֶּה, whether in number or in righteousness, is not included in the division." "And I saw that Rashi, of blessed memory, explained ״לָאֵלֶּה״ as referring to those who entered the land, according to the opinion of Rabbi Yonatan, and he said, and this is his language: ״לָאֵלֶּה״ etc., and not to those under twenty years old, even though they reached twenty before the division of the land, etc. — until here. And it is difficult: according to Rabbi Yonatan, does ״לָאֵלֶּה״ not serve to inform us that the land was divided to those who entered the land? For Rabbi Yonatan himself derived precisely this, saying: to those who entered the land the land was divided, as it is said ״לָאֵלֶּה״ etc., and what do I fulfill ״לִשְׁמוֹת״ etc. — until here. From this you have learned that he was not compelled to say 'to those who entered the land' except from the statement ״לָאֵלֶּה״, and by force of this he needed to seek what to fulfill in the statement ״לִשְׁמוֹת״ etc. From this you have learned that ״לָאֵלֶּה״ is needed to inform us that the land shall be divided to those who entered it." "<b>Yet it is true</b> that at the core of what Rashi says — that it should not be divided except to those aged twenty and up — I do not take a definitive position, even though it was not stated explicitly in the words of Rabbi Yonatan. Nevertheless, it is possible that this is subsumed within the word לָאֵלֶּה and that it bears both interpretations, except that there is no necessity for this, for perhaps it comes only to exclude those who came out of Egypt, and the matter is balanced. But one must investigate why Rashi did not explain according to the truth of the matter following Rabbi Yonatan's view." "<b>And I saw</b> that Rabbi Elijah Mizrachi explained Rashi's words as analyzing the word לָאֵלֶּה, and he did not fulfill the obligation of his pure analysis even regarding the difficulty that exists upon Rashi from the baraita. And I say that Rashi, of blessed memory, did not have the intent of Rabbi Yonatan's words hidden from him; rather, it seemed to his pure mind that from the word לָאֵלֶּה it is proven that the land was not divided except to those who were present then, and specifically to those who were aged twenty and up in that count. And this novel point is what he came to inform us, and the matter is self-evident that it was divided to those who entered the land." "<b>By the number of names.</b> That is, the division should be according to the number of names mentioned, which are fifty-seven families. The intention is that they divide the land into fifty-seven families, each family according to what it is. This approach is only according to Rabbi Yonatan, for according to Rabbi Yoshiya, who holds that the division is for those who left Egypt, one cannot divide into fifty-seven families, since one must also divide for the six families that were lacking from those who left Egypt, as stated in their words, may their memory be for blessing (Numbers Rabbah 21), and the tribe from which they were lacking shall take their portion in the law of inheritance. And it is possible that even Rabbi Yoshiya [read: Yonatan?] would justify saying ״by the number of names״ regarding the families of those who left Egypt."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {5 "The bracketed correction [צ״ל יונתן?] appears in the source; the logical difficulty is that the second Tanna should be Yoshiya (who divides by those who left Egypt), yet the text questions whether the name should read Yonatan instead — likely a copyist's confusion, translated as found."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:15:13.706457801Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}